## One country, several climates

Morocco brings together several climate zones in a fairly compact area:

- **the Atlantic coast** (Essaouira, Rabat, Casablanca), softened by the ocean, with moderate summers and mild winters;
- **the Mediterranean north** and the Rif (Tangier, Chefchaouen), greener and wetter in winter;
- **the inland plains and cities** (Marrakech, Fez, Meknes), hot in summer and cool on winter nights;
- **the Atlas mountains**, cold in winter, with snow on the peaks and sometimes on the passes;
- **the pre-Saharan south and the desert**, very hot in summer, with big temperature differences between day and night.

A single trip can take you from one climate to another in a few hours' drive, so pack clothes you can layer.

## Spring and autumn: the most versatile seasons

Spring and autumn are generally the most pleasant times for a trip that combines several regions. Temperatures are milder in the inland cities, and days in the desert are easier to bear than in high summer.

In spring, the valleys and the foothills of the Atlas are often greener. In autumn, the heat gradually eases after the summer.

{% tip title="Good to know" %}
These seasons are also in high demand. Book popular riads and desert camps in advance, especially during European school holidays.
{% /tip %}

## Summer: head for the coast and the mountains

In summer, the heat can be intense in Marrakech, Fez and the south. It is the ideal season for the Atlantic coast, where the ocean air keeps temperatures down, and for hiking at altitude in the Atlas.

If you visit the inland cities in summer, plan your days around the heat: sightseeing early in the morning and late in the day, with a break in the shade during the hottest hours.

{% warning title="The desert in summer" %}
Days in the desert can be gruelling in summer. If you go at this time of year, drink regularly, protect yourself from the sun and follow your operator's advice on when to do activities.
{% /warning %}

## Winter: sunshine in the cities, snow in the Atlas

Winter is often sunny in the south and in Marrakech, with mild days. Nights, however, are cool or even cold, especially in the desert and the mountains. Heating can be limited in traditional houses and some riads, so ask before you book.

High in the Atlas, snow transforms the landscape. It can also slow down or temporarily close some mountain passes: check road conditions before crossing the mountains and keep your plans flexible.

## Other dates to keep in mind

Beyond the weather, a few dates affect the atmosphere and the logistics of a trip:

- **Ramadan**, whose dates follow the lunar calendar and move forward by about eleven days each year (see [Travelling in Morocco during Ramadan](/en/guides/travelling-in-morocco-during-ramadan));
- **major religious holidays and school holidays**, when transport and accommodation are in high demand;
- **festivals and moussems**, which bring certain towns to life at specific times: their dates change from year to year and should be checked with the organisers.

To plan the rest of your trip, read our [two-week itinerary](/en/guides/two-week-morocco-itinerary).
